**Goal:** connect a [Worker](/lore/workers-answer-requests-durable-objects-own-named-state) to a resource it declares, and see what it gets back.

A binding is a declared connection. On Cloudflare, it lets a Worker use a platform resource such as a bucket, a queue, or a stream. [Cloudflare bindings](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/bindings/)
In [Alchemy](/skills/learn-alchemy), the binding is declared in the same [Effect](/lore/effect-basics) program as the resource.

## Worked example: the events cartridge writes to a stream

The events [cartridge](/lore/cartridges) sends one row per request to a Pipelines stream.
It binds the stream in two steps:

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/events/src/basin.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=56-57 {57} title="Init: ask for a writer bound to the stream"

```

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/events/src/basin.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=75 title="Runtime: provide the binding implementation"

```

1. `Cloudflare.Pipelines.WriteStream(stream)` names the stream this code writes to. It returns a writer with a `send` method.
2. `Layer.provide(Cloudflare.Pipelines.WriteStreamBinding)` gives the [Layer](/lore/layers-make-dependencies-explicit) what it needs: it supplies the implementation that talks to the bound stream at runtime.

Alchemy documents the same two steps for queues: `WriteQueue` in the init phase, and `WriteQueueBinding` in the runtime layer. [Queue.ts](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/v2.0.0-beta.85/packages/cloudflare/src/Queues/Queue.ts)

## Why a binding carries permission and configuration

Sam says a binding includes both permissions and environment variables.
A [service](/lore/services-capture-dependencies)'s configuration and permissions can be declared together before the application runs. [Happy Path 116, 37:09](https://youtu.be/YEn4A7XxgF4?t=2229) [37:33](https://youtu.be/YEn4A7XxgF4?t=2253)

He also describes deriving least-privilege policies: permissions limited to the methods an application uses. [Effect Office Hours 27, 58:16](https://youtu.be/fPSxB7ZgSJw?t=3496) [59:23](https://youtu.be/fPSxB7ZgSJw?t=3563)

